Equivalently to the density definition, a Gaussian random variable is any random variable of the form
$
  X ~ mu + sigma Z quad quad "with" quad Z ~ N(0, 1), mu in RR, sigma >= 0.
$
When $sigma > 0$ this has density $1 \/ sqrt(2 ppi sigma^2) exp(- (x - mu)^2 / (2 sigma^2))$; allowing $sigma = 0$ also admits the degenerate case $X = mu$.
